 or unargued verdicts, political or strategic views and typical claims that a firm leads or competes or that demand is strong or supply tight are one rejected class, common knowledge an analyst following the company already has, unless it gives a figure and a specific account. Version 29 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v28 answers): entries identical to version 28; role-v29 names the company's own leases beside its own borrowing as not an obligation; v28 opened a binding-obligation question on the company's own office lease after the guarantee wording named leases. Version 28 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v27 answers and the model's own account on the 8-K page): entries identical to version 27; role-v28 says whose obligation a guarantee or credit support covers (another party's debt, lease or revenue); v27 opened a binding-obligation question on an officer's standard indemnity agreement. Version 27 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v26 answers): entries identical to version 26; role-v27 counts one reported measure with its growth rates and share as one evidence item, as one deal or hire already is (standard section 8, judgement 11: Google Cloud's three numbers are one item); v26 split one segment's revenue, share, yearly and quarterly growth into four items. Version 26 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v25 answers and the model's own account on three pages): entries identical to version 25; role-v26 narrows the other-company rejection to another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets (v25 dropped an industry memory-spending estimate under the broader wording), names input costs among what a counterparty's action bears on, scopes a named party's claim to the company or its industry, keeps the news a share-price move reacts to, and makes the rejection list hold even when a named party states the detail (v25 kept technical throughput figures as named-party claims). Version 25 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v24 answers and the 