uestion on an officer's standard indemnity agreement. Version 27 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v26 answers): entries identical to version 26; role-v27 counts one reported measure with its growth rates and share as one evidence item, as one deal or hire already is (standard section 8, judgement 11: Google Cloud's three numbers are one item); v26 split one segment's revenue, share, yearly and quarterly growth into four items. Version 26 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v25 answers and the model's own account on three pages): entries identical to version 25; role-v26 narrows the other-company rejection to another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets (v25 dropped an industry memory-spending estimate under the broader wording), names input costs among what a counterparty's action bears on, scopes a named party's claim to the company or its industry, keeps the news a share-price move reacts to, and makes the rejection list hold even when a named party states the detail (v25 kept technical throughput figures as named-party claims). Version 25 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v24 answers and the model's own account on two pages): entries identical to version 24; role-v25 splits the binding obligation so that a guarantee, credit support, financing to others, a purchase commitment or a backstop opens whether or not the item gives its amount, while an equity stake opens only when its size is stated and it is not yet completed (v24 opened one on an unsized, completed stake); a launch or ship date opens only for the company's own product; the company's own claim to beat rivals is a product detail; rumour is for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed, a reported announcement or completed deal is a fact; four sentences that restate the schema or name a list the request does not show are cut. Version 24 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v23 bench answers): entries identical to version 23; role-v24 names the item's