src/hivemind/prompts/categories-v34.json:2: "_note": "Version 34 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v33 answers): entries identical to version 33; role-v34 removes the rejection added in v30 to v32 (an integration, paper or award without business scale): it contradicts the standard's kind 2 (a partner's action with a name is kept) and its rule that incomplete, unofficial or distant information is still evidence, its weight judged apart (Alex, 2026-10-29: \u4e0d\u8981\u8fd9\u4e48\u6b7b\u677f ... \u4f60\u5e94\u8be5\u628a\u5b83\u8bb0\u5f55\u4e0b\u6765\uff0c\u53ea\u662f\u53ef\u80fd\u6863\u4f4d\u4f4e\u4e00\u70b9). Version 33 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v32 answers): entries identical to version 32; role-v33 names a customer's, supplier's, competitor's or partner's reported figure beside its action as kind (2), with two examples (a customer's order backlog; a supplier's gross margin on higher prices), as the standard's kind 2 example does (a customer's quarterly revenue, growth and backlog); v32 dropped a memory supplier's reported results, the model reading the subject line as limiting evidence to the company (its own account). Version 32 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v31 answers): entries identical to version 31; role-v32 narrows 'a research or award result' to 'a published paper or an award'; in v31 the model read a competitor developing its own chip as an unscaled research result (its own account, page on the competitor's chip report). Version 31 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v30 answers): entries identical to version 30; role-v31 narrows v30's rejection to an integration of the company's software, models or tools into others' products or research, or a research or award result, without business scale, and names a customer's purchase, order or deployment and a choice of supplier or rival as actions; v30's wording (adoption, partnership, a hospital chain running the vendor's software) dropped a bank choosing a rival's chips, a cloud's deployment of the company's systems and the company's choice of a supplier's CPU. Version 30 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v29 answers): entries identical to version 29; role-v30 rejects an adoption, integration, partnership or research result that gives no business scale even when well-known names take part (standard section 2: product details without business scale); v29 kept about 55 such records on the 37 articles whose reference keeps nothing. Version 29 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v28 answers): entries identical to version 28; role-v29 names the company's own leases beside its own borrowing as not an obligation; v28 opened a binding-obligation question on the company's own office lease after the guarantee wording named leases. Version 28 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v27 answers and the model's own account on the 8-K page): entries identical to version 27; role-v28 says whose obligation a guarantee or credit support covers (another party's debt, lease or revenue); v27 opened a binding-obligation question on an officer's standard indemnity agreement. Version 27 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v26 answers): entries identical to version 26; role-v27 counts one reported measure with its growth rates and share as one evidence item, as one deal or hire already is (standard section 8, judgement 11: Google Cloud's three numbers are one item); v26 split one segment's revenue, share, yearly and quarterly growth into four items. Version 26 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v25 answers and the model's own account on three pages): entries identical to version 25; role-v26 narrows the other-company rejection to another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets (v25 dropped an industry memory-spending estimate under the broader wording), names input costs among what a counterparty's action bears on, scopes a named party's claim to the company or its industry, keeps the news a share-price move reacts to, and makes the rejection list hold even when a named party states the detail (v25 kept technical throughput figures as named-party claims). Version 25 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v24 answers and the model's own account on two pages): entries identical to version 24; role-v25 splits the binding obligation so that a guarantee, credit support, financing to others, a purchase commitment or a backstop opens whether or not the item gives its amount, while an equity stake opens only when its size is stated and it is not yet completed (v24 opened one on an unsized, completed stake); a launch or ship date opens only for the company's own product; the company's own claim to beat rivals is a product detail; rumour is for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed, a reported announcement or completed deal is a fact; four sentences that restate the schema or name a list the request does not show are cut. Version 24 (2026-10-29, direct read of the v23 bench answers): entries identical to version 23; role-v24 names the item's subjects as the company, keeps another company's action only when it bears on the company's demand, supply, competition, prices or products, rejects another company's own valuation, profit or unrelated deals, a retail price or content detail, an investor's trade or the market value and unargued verdicts (standard.md section 2), tells one deal, hire or programme with its figures as one evidence item (standard section 8, judgement 11), and opens the already-taken-on obligation whether or not the item gives an amount; the duplicate links sentence is cut (the payload states it). Version 23 (2026-10-29, D237): the request is scrubbed for one company or one industry (a general hedge-fund brain): T1 drops 'such as data center and gaming', T2 reads 'trade restrictions and tariffs' for 'export controls and tariffs'; role-v23 restates every example across industries (none names a covered company or comes from a test article) and carries the standard's rules of D236 (main driver includes the launch