-Evidence: what to keep
-- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: "same-store sales rose 4.2% in the quarter"; "management guided full-year revenue to $12–13 billion"; "the board approved a $10 billion buyback". (2) A customer's, supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand, supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: "the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year"; "a hospital network signed a seven-year contract for the system"; "the main supplier said its capacity will double by 2027"; "a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe"; "the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled to $40 billion". (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its industry: "the CFO said plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity"; "an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10"; "a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables by $2 billion"; "an analyst valued the shares at $80, 15 times next year's earnings of $5.30". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or step: "the regulator approved the drug for a second indication"; "the government required licences for exports of the product".
-- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last. For each statement, name its kind and check it against the list below; take it only when it has a kind and nothing on the list applies. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight such statements yields eight evidence items, not a selection.
-- The list: reject these, even when a named party states them, and everything that has no kind:
-  - a product feature, performance, compatibility, retail price or content detail that gives no business scale (units, revenue, customers or capacity), even with a figure ("the phone weighs 180 grams"; "the update makes reports load 30% faster"; "a retailer marks one model down by $50"), unless it is a competitor's claim against the company's product (the company's own claim to beat rivals is still a product detail); a partner's or customer's adoption or integration, and a change to the plan, configuration or timing of the product that carries the company's value, are not product details.
-  - an investor's position or trade, a share-price move or the market value ("he is short the stock"; "shares fell 9% this week"), though the news a move reacts to is kept.
-  - another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets ("a rival's price-to-earnings ratio"; "an unrelated merger in the same news digest").
-  - common knowledge an analyst following the company already has, unless it gives a figure and a specific account: framing or background, general risks or unargued verdicts, political or strategic views (an official's order, law, bill or ruling is kind (4), not a view), typical claims that a firm leads or competes or that demand is strong or supply tight ("rivals have spent billions but still lag"; "the company was founded in 1998"; "if demand slows it would hurt"; "the stock is a hold"; "the airline expects travel to stay strong").
-  - the same fact or arrangement said again in other words: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item ("a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years"; "cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total").
-  - a quote that only characterizes ("a pivotal moment"; "an industry-leading platform").
-  - company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.
+Evidence: how you decide
+- Read the item from its first paragraph to its last. For each statement, write two things before anything else, and keep the statement only when you can write both:
+  1. new_in_it: what is new here, as a fact. A new event or step, a new figure, a new forecast or plan, a new policy step, a new specific judgement with its reason. Not new: an arrangement the item only describes as in place (a partnership, a customer using the product, a deployment) with no new step, even when no date is given; the company's history; a claim everyone following the company makes (that it leads, that demand is strong, that supply is tight) without a figure and a specific account; a figure already known. Guidance, and a forecast or a plan still in force, are kept when retold.
+  2. moves: the question whose answer this makes more or less likely, and through which path: the company's demand, its supply and input costs, competition and share, pricing and installed base, product progress, policy, or its capital and obligations. Name an open question by its id in links when one fits; name your own candidate's id when the statement raises a new question; when it moves a question about a customer, supplier, competitor or regulator that is not open and is too small to open, write the question in moves and leave links empty. A statement that moves no question is not evidence, however specific.
+- These are not kept, whatever they look like: a product, game, driver or benchmark detail with no business scale, and a comparison the company, a partner or a third party publishes (a competitor's comparison against the company's product is judged like any other statement); a share-price move, an investor's position or an unargued view ("the stock is a hold"; a quote that only characterises); a political or strategic stance with no figure and no decision (an official's order, law or ruling is a policy step and is kept); another company's valuation, share price or deal that touches none of the company's markets; a named analyst's reading of an event already recorded that adds no fact or calculation (a conditional valuation with its assumptions, derivation and date is kept); the same fact told again in other words (one deal, hire, programme or reported measure with its terms, figures and growth rates is one evidence item); boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.
+- A page with eight statements that pass yields eight evidence items, not a selection; a page with none yields none.
 
 How to write evidence
 - One evidence item records one statement. Copy its quote verbatim, without translation. Separately write a standalone summary in one_line: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring sentence gives it.
